PRETABLE_OPUS_P8 ADDENDUM 1 -- 15:18, written as a separate file so the block's own mtime stays the order proof.

APPARATUS FAILURE, RECORDED RATHER THAN TIDIED AWAY.  Table run 1 (driver_p8.run-151417.sh) reached BASE = 46/46
and then ABORTED at the CENSUS build.  Cause: the carrier's documented Bash -> heredoc -> Python backslash trap --
the mutator's `]\n"' was written through a quoted heredoc, ONE backslash level was consumed, and the emitted C
carried a REAL NEWLINE inside a string literal ("missing terminating \" character" at cpu_m88k.c:693).  Fixed the
prescribed way (BS = chr(92), built by concatenation), dry-run against a copy of the baseline before relaunch, and
the emitted C inspected by eye.  No product tree was touched; the clone was restored from SEC and its sentinel
cleared before relaunch, verified by cmp.

TWO THINGS THIS IS NOT, and both were checked rather than assumed:
 * NOT the contention.  The failure is a compile error in the mutant source, reproducible off-line, with nothing
   timing-dependent about it.
 * NOT a finding about the fix.  BASE was 46/46 on the unmutated clone before the census step ran, so the tree and
   the instrument were both healthy.

The build FRESHNESS check ([ binary -nt source ]) is what caught it: `make' had already printed its errors, but the
driver would have gone on to score a STALE binary as the census run had the check not been there.  Recorded because
that is the exact failure the two-part clearance exists for, met in a scratch clone at table time.

PREDICTIONS ARE UNCHANGED.  Nothing about the table's design moved; only the mutator's quoting.
